          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A patient with acute anterior uveitis shows small, fine, stellate keratic precip</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient with acute anterior uveitis shows small, fine, stellate keratic precipitates on the corneal endothelium with no iris nodules. Which disease association is most likely?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Sarcoidosis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Sarcoidosis causes granulomatous uveitis characterised by large mutton-fat KPs and iris nodules (Koeppe or Busacca), not fine stellate precipitates.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Tuberculosis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Tuberculosis produces granulomatous inflammation with mutton-fat KPs and iris nodules, not fine stellate precipitates.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>HLA-B27 spondyloarthropathy</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Fine stellate precipitates without iris nodules characterise non-granulomatous iridocyclitis, which is most commonly associated with HLA-B27 spondyloarthropathies.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Vogt-Koyanagi-Harada syndrome</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>VKH causes granulomatous panuveitis with mutton-fat KPs, not the fine stellate pattern seen here.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Fine stellate KPs without nodules point to non-granulomatous disease, most commonly HLA-B27-associated. Granulomatous causes (sarcoidosis, TB, VKH) produce mutton-fat KPs and iris nodules."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. Non-granulomatous iridocyclitis shows fine stellate KPs without iris nodules and is strongly associated with HLA-B27 spondyloarthropathies such as ankylosing spondylitis."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
