          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 62-year-old diabetic patient with no history of trauma presents with a spontan</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 62-year-old diabetic patient with no history of trauma presents with a spontaneous horizontal red fluid level in the anterior chamber. Which condition should be suspected?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Endophthalmitis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Endophthalmitis produces a white hypopyon (leucocytes and fibrin), not a red hyphema (blood).</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Rubeosis iridis</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Spontaneous hyphema in a diabetic suggests rubeosis iridis — neovascularisation of the iris from retinal ischaemia. Other causes include iris melanoma and Fuchs heterochromic iridocyclitis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Behçet disease</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Behçet disease characteristically causes a sterile white hypopyon, not a red hyphema.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Severe iridocyclitis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Severe iridocyclitis causes a sterile white hypopyon from leucocyte accumulation, not a red blood-filled hyphema.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "A red fluid level is hyphema (blood), not hypopyon (white cells). Spontaneous hyphema without trauma suggests rubeosis iridis, iris melanoma, or Fuchs heterochromic iridocyclitis."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. Spontaneous hyphema (red blood level) without trauma points to abnormal iris vessels — rubeosis iridis is the leading cause, especially in diabetics with retinal ischaemia."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
