          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>In this patient's presentation, which single finding most critically differentia</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>In this patient's presentation, which single finding most critically differentiates iridocyclitis from acute angle-closure glaucoma?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Circumciliary congestion</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Circumciliary congestion can occur in both iridocyclitis and acute angle-closure glaucoma, so it does not reliably differentiate between them.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Photophobia</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Photophobia may be present in both conditions and is not the key differentiating feature.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Posterior synechiae</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Posterior synechiae are characteristic of iridocyclitis but intraocular pressure is the more critical differentiating parameter in the acute setting.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Low intraocular pressure</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Low IOP is the hallmark distinction: the inflamed ciliary body secretes less aqueous, lowering pressure. In acute angle-closure glaucoma, IOP is markedly elevated — missing this one finding means missing the diagnosis entirely.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The most critical differentiator is intraocular pressure: low in iridocyclitis (reduced aqueous secretion) versus very high in acute angle-closure glaucoma. Missing the low IOP means missing the classification."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. Low IOP from reduced aqueous secretion by the inflamed ciliary body is the defining distinction from acute angle-closure glaucoma, where IOP is markedly elevated."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
